                <PostCover slug="spec-drift" category="Architecture" cover="docs-to-checks" variant="hero" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="sb-shell">
        <div class="sb-frame sb-body">
          <div class="sb-main">
            <details class="sb-toc-mobile">
              <summary>
                On this page
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" /></svg>
              </summary>
              <ol>
                {toc.map((heading) => (
                  <li><a href={`#${heading.id}`}>{heading.text}</a></li>
                ))}
              </ol>
            </details>

            <div class="sb-prose" data-sb-prose>
              <h2 id="what-is-spec-drift">What is spec drift?</h2>
              <p>
                <strong>Spec drift</strong> is the gap that opens when the code no longer does what its committed
                specs, design docs, ADRs or agent instruction files say it does. It almost never arrives as a
                decision. A refactor moves a call into a new class, a cleanup deletes a type, a quick fix reaches
                across a module boundary. The pull request is reviewed on its diff, the document is not in the diff,
                and from the merge on the sentence describes code that no longer exists.
              </p>
              <p>
                Nothing fails when that happens. Tests check behaviour, and the behaviour is usually fine. The
                document is simply wrong, and it stays wrong until someone happens to read it closely enough to
                notice, which for most architecture docs is never.
              </p>

              <h2 id="why-agents-make-it-worse">Why coding agents make it worse</h2>
              <p>
                A stale document used to cost a new contributor an afternoon. Now it has a second reader that does
                not get suspicious. Coding agents are handed <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, specs
                and design docs as context and treat them as instructions. When a sentence says
                <code>NodeWorker</code> calls <code>RepairScheduler</code> and the code says otherwise, the quickest way
                for an agent to make the sentence true again is to re-create the dependency your last refactor removed.
                A stale spec is a set of instructions for undoing your own work.
              </p>
              <p>
                Agents also change more code, faster, than the docs were ever reviewed against. In
                <a href="/blog/architecture-matters-more-not-less/">our survey of 335 open-source repositories</a> that
                describe their architecture in their own docs, sixty-three percent give their coding agents documents
                to build from. Of those, 8% run any tool that checks the code against an architecture rule.
              </p>
              <div class="bp-keystat bp-keystat--danger" data-reveal>
                <div class="bp-keystat-value">8%</div>
                <div class="bp-keystat-body">
                  of the repositories that hand their agents docs to build from <b>run anything that checks the code against an architecture rule</b>.
                  <span class="bp-keystat-src">Striff survey of 335 repositories, September 2026</span>
                </div>
              </div>

              <h2 id="what-it-looks-like">What spec drift looks like in real repositories</h2>
              <p>
                Spec drift comes in a few recurring shapes. Each example below is a public repository&rsquo;s own
                document against its own code, with links to the line and the commit, so you can check it without
                taking our word for it.
              </p>

              <h3>A refactor moves the work, and the doc still credits the old class</h3>
              <p>
                Ericsson&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/Ericsson/ecchronos">ecChronos</a> documents its
                <code>core.impl</code> module class by class. Of <code>NodeWorker</code> the README says: &ldquo;Calls
                <code>RepairScheduler.putConfigurations()</code> to keep jobs up to date.&rdquo; Pull request
                <a href="https://github.com/Ericsson/ecchronos/pull/1786">#1786</a> moved that call into a new class,
                <code>SchemaRefresher</code>, and after the change <code>NodeWorker</code> no longer references
                <code>RepairScheduler</code> at all. The refactor was fine. The README was not in the diff.
                <a href="/blog/design-docs-are-enforceable-now/">The full walk-through</a> shows the rule, both
                revisions and the line that moved.
              </p>

              <h3>The exemplar breaks the rule it is praised for</h3>
              <p>
                In <a href="https://github.com/catatafishen/agentbridge">agentbridge</a>, an IDE plugin that gives coding
                agents access to the IDE, <code>AGENTS.md</code> has a section called &ldquo;Exemplary Patterns Already in the
                Codebase&rdquo;. It points agents at <code>MessageFormatter.kt</code> as &ldquo;pure formatting
                utilities&rdquo; with &ldquo;Zero UI dependencies&rdquo;. Since a
                <a href="https://github.com/catatafishen/agentbridge/commit/b55868a9e5">refactor in May 2026</a> that moved
                shared types out of the <code>ui</code> package, the file imports
                <code>com.github.catatafishen.agentbridge.ui.toolSubtitleKey</code>.
              </p>
              <div class="bp-figure" data-reveal>
                <p class="bp-figure-title">catatafishen/agentbridge · already broken on master</p>
                <div class="bp-facts">
                  <div class="bp-facts-line"><span class="bp-facts-key">doc   </span>AGENTS.md, &ldquo;Exemplary Patterns Already in the Codebase&rdquo;</div>
                  <div class="bp-facts-line"><span class="bp-facts-key">says  </span><span class="bp-facts-quote">"MessageFormatter.kt — pure formatting utilities … Zero UI dependencies."</span></div>
                  <div class="bp-facts-line"><span class="bp-facts-key">rule  </span>MessageFormatter may not depend on com.github.catatafishen.agentbridge.ui</div>
                  <div class="bp-facts-line"><span class="bp-facts-key">code  </span>MessageFormatter.kt:3 imports agentbridge.ui.toolSubtitleKey</div>
                  <div class="bp-facts-line"><span class="bp-facts-flag">✗ ALREADY BROKEN</span>  false before the pull request and after it</div>
                </div>
                <p class="bp-figure-caption">
                  From Striff&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/catatafishen/agentbridge/runs/122211791267">check on pull request #1201</a>,
                  October 27, 2026. The pull request did not cause it, so the check lists it apart from the nine rules the change
                  was held to, as already broken on the default branch.
                </p>
              </div>
              <p>
                This is the drift that matters most for agents. The document does not merely describe the code; it
                tells the next agent which file to copy. Copying it now reproduces the dependency the document says
                is absent.
              </p>

              <h3>The doc names a class the code deleted</h3>
              <p>
                The same repository&rsquo;s <code>docs/KNOWLEDGE-GRAPH.md</code> still lists
                <code>KnowledgeGraphPanel</code> as the settings UI. The class was removed on June 10, 2026
                (<a href="https://github.com/catatafishen/agentbridge/commit/e99aae3174d6e7f1298dc3925229e1bccab487b3">e99aae3</a>),
                and <code>docs/ACCEPTED-API-WARNINGS.md</code> still lists three tools deleted a month earlier
                (<a href="https://github.com/catatafishen/agentbridge/commit/dce714488c9aeee98af644f0530369818e4fb33c">dce7144</a>).
                Names that outlive their classes are the most common kind of spec drift and the cheapest to fix.
              </p>
              <p>
                In <a href="https://github.com/objectionary/eo">objectionary/eo</a>, the Maven plugin&rsquo;s README said
                <code>org.eolang.parser.Program</code> runs the XSLT transformations and <code>org.eolang.parser.Pack</code>
                holds the list of them. Neither class exists any more. After Striff&rsquo;s report on the repository listed
                both names as out of date, the maintainer replaced them with <code>Pipeline</code> and <code>Canonical</code>
                in <a href="https://github.com/objectionary/eo/commit/9c2adafbc83580dd15f29a43f8b5d032ada15538">one commit</a>.
                The <a href="/objectionary/eo/">public report on eo</a> shows every rule its docs state today.
              </p>

              <h3>How often it happens</h3>
              <p>
                Rarely per pull request, which is exactly why nobody notices. Across 609 public pull requests in 542
                repositories, Striff read 7,161 rules out of those repositories&rsquo; own documentation and could answer
                5,674 of them from the parsed code. Roughly two percent of the answered rules accused: 97 broken by the
                change under review, 53 already broken before it.
                <a href="/blog/design-docs-are-enforceable-now/">The numbers and the method</a> are in the original post,
                with five disagreements from large repositories checked by hand.
              </p>

              <h2 id="spec-driven-development">Spec drift in spec-driven development</h2>
              <p>
                Spec-driven development comes in roughly three levels. In <em>spec-first</em> work, a spec drives one
                task and can be thrown away when the task ends, so it cannot drift in any way that matters. In
                <em>spec-anchored</em> work, the spec is kept after the task and every later change is made from it.
                In <em>spec-as-source</em> work, people edit only the spec and never the code.
              </p>
              <p>
                Spec drift is the failure mode of the second level, and most teams are at the second level whether
                they call it that or not: a repository that hands its agents <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>
                or architecture docs is spec-anchored in practice. The spec outlives the task, the next task starts
                from it, and the workflows that help you write a spec and build from it do not check, on the pull
                requests that follow, that the code still matches it.
              </p>

              <h2 id="detect-by-hand">How to detect spec drift by hand</h2>
              <p>
                If you want to know how far one document has drifted today, read it with the code open beside it and
                check only the sentences that make a claim about structure:
              </p>
              <ol>
                <li><strong>Names.</strong> Search the code for every class, module and function the document names. A name that only appears in docs is drift.</li>
                <li><strong>Locations.</strong> Where the document says something lives (&ldquo;the parsers live in <code>core.parse</code>&rdquo;), open that package.</li>
                <li><strong>Dependency directions.</strong> For every &ldquo;A calls B&rdquo; or &ldquo;A uses B&rdquo;, check the imports of A.</li>
                <li><strong>&ldquo;Must not&rdquo; rules.</strong> For every ban (&ldquo;the core never imports from plugins&rdquo;), search for the forbidden import. These are the rules an agent in a hurry breaks first.</li>
                <li><strong>Exemplars.</strong> Every file an agent file holds up as a model to copy, check against the property it is praised for.</li>
              </ol>
              <p>
                Then do it again after every merge that touches those files, which is where the manual approach stops
                working. <a href="/blog/architecture-review-checklist/">The pull request review checklist</a> covers
                the by-hand version in more depth, with the arithmetic on how long it takes.
              </p>

              <h2 id="detect-on-every-pull-request">How to catch spec drift on every pull request</h2>
              <p>
                The way to keep a spec true is to check it at the moment it can go false: on the pull request, while
                the fix is still one line and the author still remembers why the call moved. That is what Striff does,
                as a GitHub check beside your tests.
              </p>
              <ol>
                <li><strong>It reads the docs you already have.</strong> Specs, design docs, ADRs, READMEs, <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. There are no rule files to write and nothing to configure.</li>
                <li><strong>It turns checkable sentences into rules.</strong> A language model proposes rules from the prose. A rule that names a class the parsed code does not contain is dropped, so a rule cannot be invented by inventing a name.</li>
                <li><strong>It checks both revisions.</strong> Each rule is evaluated against a parse of the code before and after the pull request, so the check can say which change broke it.</li>
                <li><strong>It double-checks every accusation.</strong> Before a broken rule is shown, an independent agent re-reads the document and the code, twice, and the finding is withheld unless every run confirms it.</li>
                <li><strong>It quotes the sentence.</strong> Every rule on the check links the document and line it came from, so the reviewer decides whether the code or the sentence is wrong.</li>
              </ol>
              <p>Each rule gets one of these verdicts on the pull request:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Held:</strong> nothing in this change breaks it.</li>
                <li><strong>Violated:</strong> true before this change and false after it. This change caused the drift.</li>
                <li><strong>Already broken:</strong> false before the change too. Reported, and never blamed on the author in front of you.</li>
                <li><strong>Restored:</strong> broken before, true after.</li>
                <li><strong>Couldn&rsquo;t check:</strong> the rule could not be answered from the code (it names generated code a source parse cannot see, say), or an accusation was not confirmed. It is counted and left out, never shown as passing.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                Separately, a check notes the documents that still name files a pull request deleted or renamed, which is the cheapest drift to fix at the moment it happens. On the default branch, the
                dashboard keeps every rule&rsquo;s standing, so you can see which documents have already drifted
                without waiting for a pull request to touch them. <a href="/demo/">The demo</a> shows it on an example
                repository, and public reports such as <a href="/apache/pinot/">apache/pinot</a> and
                <a href="/dotnet/benchmarkdotnet/">dotnet/benchmarkdotnet</a> show it on real ones.
                <a href="/blog/grounded-ai-findings/">How every finding traces back to your docs or your code</a> goes
                through what a check says and how to verify it.
              </p>

              <h2 id="write-checkable-docs">How to write docs that stay checkable</h2>
              <p>
                You do not have to change how you write. A README written for people produces rules. A few habits make
                more of it checkable:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Name real things.</strong> <code>NodeWorker</code> is checkable; &ldquo;the worker&rdquo; is only checkable if one class answers to it.</li>
                <li><strong>Say where things live and what they use.</strong> &ldquo;<code>ConnectionType</code> is in <code>utils</code>&rdquo; and &ldquo;<code>NodeWorker</code> calls <code>RepairScheduler</code>&rdquo; are claims a parser can answer at both revisions.</li>
                <li><strong>Write the bans down.</strong> &ldquo;The domain module must not depend on infrastructure&rdquo; becomes a rule the moment it is written.</li>
                <li><strong>Leave intent as prose.</strong> &ldquo;We prefer composition&rdquo; is not a claim about structure, and nothing should pretend to check it.</li>
                <li><strong>Treat agent files as architecture docs.</strong> The rules you give your agents are the ones most worth checking against the code they write.</li>
              </ul>

              <h2 id="faq">Spec drift FAQ</h2>
              {faqs.map((faq) => (
                <Fragment>
                  <h3>{faq.q}</h3>
                  <p set:html={faq.a} />
                </Fragment>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>

          <aside class="sb-rail" aria-label="Table of contents">
            <nav class="sb-toc">
              <p class="sb-label">On this page</p>
              <ol>
                {toc.map((heading) => (
                  <li><a href={`#${heading.id}`} data-toc-link={heading.id}>{heading.text}</a></li>
                ))}
              </ol>
            </nav>
          </aside>
        </div>
      </div>

      <footer class="sb-shell">
        <div class="sb-frame">
          <div class="sb-frame-main">
            <section class="sb-cta" aria-labelledby="sb-cta-title">
              <div>
                <p class="sb-label">The Striff GitHub App</p>
